  abstract-en: [
    Medical image classification plays an important role in disease screening and computer-aided diagnosis. However, the scarcity of high-quality labeled data and the substantial costs of storage and computation severely hinder the deployment of advanced classification models in resource-constrained settings. Dataset distillation offers a promising solution by compressing large-scale training sets into a small number of synthetic samples, yet existing methods are primarily designed for natural images and face challenges such as domain discrepancy, limited clustering strategies, and inefficient training when applied to medical images.

    This thesis proposes a medical image dataset distillation method based on LoRA fine-tuning of diffusion models and global prototype clustering. On the generation side, LoRA is employed to adapt a pre-trained diffusion model to the medical image domain with minimal trainable parameters. Global clustering is then performed in the latent space, where a majority voting mechanism assigns class labels to cluster centers, producing prototypes that capture cross-category feature sharing relationships. These prototypes are subsequently used as starting points for diffusion-based denoising to generate high-quality distilled images. On the training side, a teacher-student soft-label framework combined with a pre-computed batch processing strategy enables lightweight student models to learn effective classification capabilities under extremely compressed data conditions.

    Experiments on three public medical image datasets, namely DermaMNIST, BloodMNIST, and APTOS-2019, demonstrate that the proposed method outperforms baselines such as random selection and K-Means nearest-neighbor selection across all compression ratios. With only 200 synthetic images per class, classification accuracy approaches the upper bound of full-data training. Ablation studies reveal that global clustering and soft-label training contribute approximately 4 to 10 and 3 to 8 percentage points of performance gain, respectively. Compared with mainstream distillation methods including D4M, MGD3, and DDQO, the proposed method achieves the best results at all compression ratios. Meanwhile, the amount of training data is reduced by approximately 90%, with a corresponding significant reduction in training time.

    These results demonstrate that through domain adaptation for medical images, cross-category global clustering, and efficient soft-label training, dataset distillation can achieve a favorable balance between high compression ratios and high accuracy in medical image classification, providing a viable technical solution for medical AI deployment in resource-limited environments.
